I’m using the repository’s `crimes-codebase-risk` workflow to inspect the three implementations and base the proposed shared API on their actual differences. The supplied scan already confirms the duplication, so I’ll keep this read-only and focus on a concrete refactor plan.

The scan confirms one medium-severity near-duplicate spanning all three sanitizers:

- `src/sanitize-a.ts:10–36` — `sanitizeUserInput`
- `src/sanitize-b.ts:9–35` — `sanitizeText`
- `src/sanitize-c.ts:9–35` — `cleanMessage`

Their behavior is identical; only identifiers differ.

I’d add `src/sanitize.ts` with this public API:

```ts
export function sanitizeText(
  value: string | null | undefined,
): string;
```

Then preserve the existing exports as thin compatibility wrappers:

```ts
// sanitize-a.ts
export function sanitizeUserInput(raw: string): string {
  return sanitizeText(raw);
}

// sanitize-c.ts
export function cleanMessage(value: string): string {
  return sanitizeText(value);
}
```

`sanitize-b.ts` can directly re-export the shared function:

```ts
export { sanitizeText } from "./sanitize.js";
```

Files changed:

- Add `src/sanitize.ts` — canonical implementation.
- Edit `src/sanitize-a.ts` — delegate `sanitizeUserInput`.
- Edit `src/sanitize-b.ts` — re-export or delegate `sanitizeText`.
- Edit `src/sanitize-c.ts` — delegate `cleanMessage`.
- Add a shared sanitizer test file if this fixture gains tests.

This consolidates future behavioral changes while avoiding a breaking rename for existing callers. The admin-role helpers should remain untouched in this refactor; they belong to the separate duplicated-policy finding.
